1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:08,089
In this exercise, we train Cellpose to find
small, dark nucleoli in the course phase
contrast images.

2
00:00:08,329 --> 00:00:14,653
This is an example target. Look for the small
dark structures inside the larger nuclei.

3
00:00:14,893 --> 00:00:22,542
We will label a training region, check its
object hierarchy, and finish with a real
prediction on a separate test image.

4
00:00:23,693 --> 00:00:32,727
Add the thirty course images to a project. Run
the calibration script and set the pixel size
to zero point two micrometers per pixel.

5
00:00:32,967 --> 00:00:37,686
Use the Image tab to verify the calibration
after reopening the image.

6
00:00:37,926 --> 00:00:45,915
Use images one through fourteen for training,
fifteen through twenty for validation, and
twenty-one through thirty for testing.

7
00:00:46,155 --> 00:00:53,329
Keep every image in just one split. Leave the
test images without training or validation
annotations.

8
00:00:54,485 --> 00:01:01,524
Run the class setup script, open image zero
three, and center the viewer on the area you
want to label.

9
00:01:01,764 --> 00:01:11,213
Open Create region annotations from the
Classify menu. Set both dimensions to two
hundred and fifty-six, with Pixels as the unit.

10
00:01:11,453 --> 00:01:17,352
Choose Training and Viewer center. Click Create
region to add the outer rectangle.

11
00:01:17,592 --> 00:01:22,411
Use the same size for Validation rectangles on
the validation images.

12
00:01:23,568 --> 00:01:32,187
Turn off Auto set so the object outlines can
stay unclassified. Zoom in and use the Wand or
Brush to outline a nucleolus.

13
00:01:32,427 --> 00:01:39,441
Trace a second one as a separate object. Keep
neighboring targets separate and check the
boundary as you go.

14
00:01:39,681 --> 00:01:46,900
Work across the entire rectangle. Unlabeled
targets inside the region become background
during training.

15
00:01:47,140 --> 00:01:56,509
Use the illustrated labels to see the structure
of the set. In your own project, review each
outline and look for missed targets before
saving.

16
00:01:57,693 --> 00:02:04,782
Open the Hierarchy tab and expand Annotation,
Training. The blue row is the outer rectangle.

17
00:02:05,022 --> 00:02:12,077
Every indented row beneath it is a separate
object outline. These red annotations are
unclassified.

18
00:02:12,317 --> 00:02:18,396
Repeat the same check for each Validation
rectangle, then save your changes before
training.

19
00:02:19,568 --> 00:02:26,337
Run the training script. This demonstration
starts from scratch and uses seventy-five
epochs.

20
00:02:26,577 --> 00:02:36,801
For these small nucleoli we used a diameter of
two micrometers. For your own labels, measure
several representative objects to estimate a
diameter.

21
00:02:37,041 --> 00:02:43,840
During training, review the log and validation
loss. The weights are saved in the models
folder.

22
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:55,584
Also read the validation report. This first run
found no objects at the default prediction
thresholds. We'll adjust inference and inspect
the output on a separate image.

23
00:02:56,735 --> 00:03:02,409
Open image twenty-one. This image was held out
from training and validation.

24
00:03:02,649 --> 00:03:09,673
Draw and select a parent annotation, run the
custom model script, and choose the model you
just saved.

25
00:03:09,913 --> 00:03:21,457
Here we used a two micrometer diameter, a cell
probability threshold of minus two, and a flow
threshold of zero. A flow threshold of zero
disables that filter.

26
00:03:21,697 --> 00:03:32,791
The outlines on the right are the actual
Cellpose predictions. Compare them with the raw
image and look for misses, extra detections,
and outlines that merge two targets.
